<sn><md>### Chiral Carbon Asymmetric Carbon

* **Definition**: A carbon atom that is bonded to `four completely different atoms or groups` of atoms.
* **Carbon Numbering Convention**: Numbering starts(1) at the carbon closest to or part of the functional group (C1).
* **Identification in Glucose $C_6H_{12}O_6$**:
  ![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/Soil%20Biochemistry/Carbohydrate/assets/chiral_carbon_in_glucose.jpeg)
  * **C1**: Not chiral (double-bonded to oxygen in the aldehyde group).
  * **C2, C3, C4, C5**: **Chiral carbons** (each attached to four unique atomic groups).
  * **C6**: Not chiral (bonded to two identical hydrogen atoms).
</md></sn><sn><md>### Epimers and Epimerization

* **Epimers**: Stereoisomers that differ in configuration `around only one specific chiral carbon` atom.
  ![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/Soil%20Biochemistry/Carbohydrate/assets/epimers_example.png)
  * **D-Galactose vs. D-Glucose**: Differing configuration specifically at **C-4**.
  * **D-Glucose vs. D-Mannose**: Differing configuration specifically at **C-2**.

* **Epimerization**: The chemical process of `interconverting one epimer into another` (e.g., Glucose -&gt; Galactose or Glucose -&gt; Mannose).

</md></sn><sn><md>### Enantiomers 

D/L Sugar Notation

* **Enantiomers**: A special class of stereoisomers that are non-superimposable `mirror images` of each other.

* **D and L Designation**: Determined by the orientation of the $-OH$ group on the reference chiral carbon:
  ![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/Soil%20Biochemistry/Carbohydrate/assets/LandD_glucose_example.png)
  * **D-Sugar**: $-OH$ group is positioned on the `right side`.
  * **L-Sugar**: $-OH$ group is positioned on the `left side`.

* **Optical Activity**:
  * **Dextrorotatory $D$ or $+$**: Rotates plane-polarized `light to the `right (clockwise).
  * **Levorotatory $L$ or $-$**: Rotates plane-polarized `light to the `left` (counter-clockwise).
</md></sn><sn><md>#### Racemic Mixture 

DL Mixture

A **racemic mixture** is a ++solution++ containing `equal concentrations` (equimolar amounts) of both `D- and L-isomers`. 
Because the opposite optical rotations cancel each other out, the overall mixture exhibits `no net optical activity`.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Epimers vs. Enantiomers
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/Soil%20Biochemistry/Carbohydrate/assets/epimers_vs_enantiomer.png)
* **Epimers**: Differ `at only one chiral center`, while the rest of the molecule remains identical.
* **Enantiomers**: Differ `at all chiral centers` because the entire molecules are mirror images of each other.
</md></sn>